 ⚾️ It's Friday and I'll be living and dying with my Milwaukee Brewers in the World Series starting tonight. If they lose this series, I'll be needing some time off to recover. But for today... 

 Today's newsletter is 949 words, a 3½-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 0.5% today and finished the week down by the same amount.

 🛢️ Brent crude rose to $117 a barrel, marking its biggest weekly rise in two months. 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: CrowdStrike (+8.3%) led gains in cybersecurity stocks following warnings this week that websites around the world were vulnerable to hackers.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: AI anxiety 
 
 By Matt Phillips 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Axios/Matt Phillips Soaring sales, booming backlogs and gangbusters guidance from AI-related companies just doesn't cut the mustard in this market moment.

 Why it matters: The lackluster response to still strong AI-related earnings points to growing investor doubts about the durability of the boom. 📉 Catch up quick: A broad swath of AI-related shares took it on the chin this week, including:

 Hyperscaler Meta (down 9.4%). AI-construction plays like HVAC provider Comfort Systems (-19.3%) and engineering and construction giant Quanta (-13.5%). And AI power trades like turbine maker GE Vernova (-11.5%) and Bloom Energy (-12.7%). Between the lines: The slump came despite some of those very same companies reporting the kind of top-line Q3 results that likely would have received the market's version of a standing ovation last year.

 Cases in point: Meta's third-quarter results Wednesday were great. It was a legit beat-and-raise . The stock plunged 7% the next day.

 Western Digital, a maker of hard disk drives that suddenly became indispensable components of data centers, also reported astounding figures yesterday. EPS rocketed up by 161% year over year. And yet... crickets. The shares slipped 3% today. 🤨 Reality check: The AI trade has suffered through crises of confidence before.

 Remember the DeepSeek freak in early 2025? Yes, but: The inability of AI shares to elicit any enthusiasm from investors is a big problem for the market.

 In back-of-the-envelope terms, AI-related shares — using the tech sector plus Meta, Amazon and Alphabet as a proxy — represent more than 50% of market capitalization of the S&amp;P 500. 🔭 The bottom line: If the AI trade stops working, it's quite hard to see how the overall market keeps any kind of forward momentum. Buckle up.

