 2. The iPhone fold rush 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The first customers of the day hold up iPhone Duo smartphones at Apple's Fifth Avenue store during the first day of in-store sales in New York. Photo: Michael Nagle/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Apple iPhone Duo seekers never had a chance, Axios' Pete Gannon writes .

 State of play: Apple's first foldable iPhone went on sale in stores today in limited supply — and it reportedly went fast.

 The highly anticipated launch had Apple diehards and foldable-curious consumers lining up on streets from New York to Berlin to Beijing, reports noted . Most store supplies were tied to preorders, but a small number of devices were available for walk-ins. 📱 Why it matters: The iPhone is central to Apple's consumer hardware business, accounting for a significant share of the company's sales and supporting a vast network of suppliers and partners, Axios' Ina Fried has noted .

 And Apple hasn't been shy about pumping up the Duo, which starts at $1,999, with marketing executive Greg Joswiak calling it "the most transformational iPhone experience since the original." 📊 What we're watching: Supply constraints have limited Apple's production of the Duo, with JPMorgan analysts recently estimating around 8 million–9 million units shipped this year, down from earlier expectations of 10 million, per CNBC .

 Sales data is expected in the coming weeks, and Apple is scheduled to report quarterly earnings on Nov. 2. Can you still get one? Delivery dates for the Duo now show late January on Apple's website, though customers are being told to check back tomorrow for more store availability.

 
 
