 4. 🎬 The Holmes truth 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Elizabeth Holmes in "You Can See Everything." Photo: Faith Potter/A24

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 ‼️ Spoiler alert: This is my take on the new Elizabeth Holmes documentary. I'm steering clear of any giveaways — but if you're avoiding reading everything before "You Can See Everything," don't say I didn't warn you... 

 I joined the Cool Kids Club earlier this week and saw " You Can See Everything ," the new Elizabeth Holmes documentary.

 My verdict: It was captivating. 🎬 State of play: Here's what to expect from the film co-directed by and starring Nathan Fielder:

 You don't need to know the backstory of how Holmes was convicted on fraud charges for her reign at blood-testing startup Theranos — though it would help because the filmmakers did not attempt to relitigate the facts of the case. Holmes and her romantic partner, Billy Evans, granted unprecedented access to Fielder and co-director Lance Oppenheim in the apparent hope of humanizing her. She maintains that she did nothing wrong. 💭 My thought bubble: Everyone seems to have a different perspective on the big takeaways of the film, so here's mine:

 It was a fascinating depiction of the complicated pursuit of the truth — and what happens when we get a close-up view of people attempting to bend reality. Put it this way: I wrote an entire book on why people believe things that aren't true, and yet to see Holmes maintaining — without blinking — that she did nothing wrong was still jaw-dropping. The bottom line: It's worth the nearly three-hour runtime to judge it for yourself.

 
 
